## Summary

**What was failing:** The test `base-symlink-operand-is-followed` failed because when a symlink is passed directly as an operand, the code used `lstat()` which returns info about the symlink itself (type `S_IFLNK`), not the target. Since symlinks aren't regular files or directories, they weren't being processed.

**What I changed:** Changed `lstat()` to `stat()` at two locations in `/Users/sonjabrown/agentic_cyber/runs/formal/grep-qwen3-topk40-t0-p1-seed42-maxtok32768-loops1-n10/lineage-009/003/temp-0p0/attempt-001/workdir/src/new_grep/new_grep.c`:
- Line 286: `has_dir_operand` check
- Line 322: Operand type checking and processing

The `stat()` function follows symlinks, so when a symlink is passed as a direct operand, it returns info about the target file.

**Build command:** `mkdir -p build && cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic -O2 src/new_grep/new_grep.c -o build/new_grep`

**Test results:** All 86 tests pass (51 base + 11 no_filename + 15 recursive + 9 with_filename).
